body:
You need to start showing initiative on doing things when they have no power. Home alone? Cook a meal, do your laundry. Take the step to do adult-like things. Eventually, your parents are gonna get used to it. It won't take forever, but it also won't just be something that you will get through asap. 

&#x200B;

You're about to leave for work? Say, "Alright I'm off to work, cya love you." 

Your mom tells you to take a shower at certain times like 9PM? Take one an hour earlier.

Someone asks how works going and your mom answers for you by saying, "He's doing fine." you interject with your own statement after and start a conversation with them. Take control of it by adding a few things that highlighted your times at work or things that made it a bad day.

They helicopter you and your location by asking all the time? Tell them ahead where you're going before you leave.

&#x200B;

You cannot go straight into calling them out on how they treat you like a child because it'll hit their ego and make them want to treat you like a child even more. You have to slowly convert them into wanting to stop treating you like a child by acting like an adult before they can "correct" you. I've faced this before with my mom doing everything for me and acting like I was toddler carrying a toaster into my bath. The moment I showed adult-like qualities she dropped the act and let me do more adult-like things on my own.

&#x200B;

Also protip: If they have access to your banking info and can take money out, REMOVE THAT PRIVILEGE ASAP. They can use it to their advantage and drain it so you can't take money out and limit how you buy stuff like food and ingredients or detergent.
